#!/usr/bin/env node

// Derives the _DEPARE_EDGE layer: the shared edges of the DEPARE/DRGARE
// partition, each carrying the depths on either side of it.
//
// Usage:
//   bin/generate-depare-edges <output.geojson> \
//     --depth-area DEPARE.geojson [--depth-area DRGARE.geojson ...] \
//     [--contour DEPCNT.geojson] [--coverage quilting_coverage.geojson] \
//     [--fallback-coverage quilting_fallback_coverage.geojson] \
//     [--cell-coverage M_COVR.geojson]
//
// Why this layer exists (S-52 DEPARE03):
//
//   The safety contour is not "the DEPCNT whose VALDCO equals SAFETY_CONTOUR".
//   S-52 defines it as the boundary between safe and unsafe water, which is the
//   interface between two depth areas -- and an ENC is under no obligation to
//   carry a DEPCNT at the mariner's chosen contour at all. Drawing DEPCNT alone
//   leaves the safety contour missing whenever the mariner picks a value the
//   chart does not happen to have a contour for, and leaves dredged areas
//   (DRGARE, which has DRVAL1 but no surrounding DEPCNT) with no edge whatever.
//
// Each output feature carries:
//
//   DRVAL_LO   DRVAL1 of the shallower side; -1 where the other side is not a
//              depth area at all (land, unsurveyed, or the edge of coverage)
//   DRVAL_HI   DRVAL1 of the deeper side
//   VALDCO     the value of a DEPCNT that runs along this edge, when there is
//              one, so the style can label it
//   SEAM       1 when the edge is an artefact of quilting rather than a real
//              interface (see below); DRVAL_LO is then absent
//
// The edges are recovered by hashing segments rather than by a geometric
// overlay: adjacent S-57 areas are built from the same edge primitives, so a
// shared boundary comes out of ogr2ogr as bit-identical coordinate pairs in
// both polygons. A segment owned by two areas is an interface; one owned by a
// single area is an outer boundary.
//
// Three things make an outer boundary that is NOT the edge of the survey, and
// all of them have to be recognised or the style paints a safety contour along
// them.
//
//   1. The quilt clip. bin/s57-to-tiles cuts this chart against the coverage
//      of the charts it hands over to, so a depth area cut by that clip
//      acquires a new outer boundary along the cut. The detailed chart
//      continues there. --coverage carries those M_COVR rings, and it holds
//      strictly FINER coverage: a stamped copy is cut against nothing else.
//
//   2. The cell's OWN edge. An ENC cell is a rectangle cut out of a continuous
//      survey: the depth areas along its border are truncated by the cut, and
//      the neighbouring cell carries the rest. This is by far the commoner
//      case -- measured on the Seattle z11 tile, 169 of 172 edges lying on the
//      cell ring were being labelled DRVAL_LO = -1, i.e. "land", which drew
//      the safety contour down every cell edge in the quilt. --cell-coverage
//      carries this cell's own M_COVR, and only the OUTER ring of its
//      CATCOV = 1 (coverage available) polygons is indexed -- a CATCOV = 2 gap
//      is normally a hole in that polygon, and indexing the hole would put the
//      no-coverage boundary straight back in.
//
//      M_COVR is EXEMPT from the zoom partition (it is the quilt's own
//      pick/status layer), so that ring now arrives UNCLIPPED, and it has to:
//      the whole copy of a depth area is uncut and ends at the cell's own
//      border even where a finer chart overlaps that border, and a ring
//      clipped away there would read those edges as land. The cut boundaries
//      the ladder copies do have are recognised by case 1 alone.
//
//   3. The FALLBACK copy's clip. A cell that has to stand in for a chart that
//      is not there is also published BELOW its own quilt floor, as a fallback
//      continuation (_QFALL = 1), and that copy is cut against the union of
//      all strictly COARSER coverage -- so it acquires new outer boundaries
//      too, at the compilation-scale junction rather than at the finer one.
//      --fallback-coverage carries those rings.
//
//      They are consulted on the _QFALL interval ALONE. No other copy was ever
//      cut against a coarser chart, so on a stamped copy a coarser ring lies
//      over ground no clip touched -- it crosses the cell's interior freely --
//      and letting it flag a seam would take a real contour off the chart.
//
// However the boundary arose, the segment is flagged SEAM and left without a
// DRVAL_LO.
//
// BOTH endpoints and the midpoint must lie on the ring, whichever source it
// came from. A coastline that merely touches a ring at a single vertex -- a
// headland running down to the border -- is a real land boundary and must keep
// DRVAL_LO = -1; testing the midpoint alone is enough to flag any segment whose
// middle happens to graze one. The coverage test used to do exactly that, and
// got away with it only because --coverage holds strictly finer charts, whose
// rings can appear inside this cell only where it was cut. Coarser rings, which
// --fallback-coverage does hold, cross this cell wherever the coarser chart
// happens to run -- which is why the interval gate above is not on its own
// enough, and the along-the-ring rule matters most there.
//
// THE ZOOM PARTITION
//
// A partitioned cell arrives as zoom-range COPIES of each depth area -- one per
// interval of the copy ladder, carrying _QZMIN/_QZMAX (and _QFALL on a fallback
// continuation) -- which bin/stamp-quilt-zooms turns into per-feature tippecanoe
// zoom bounds after this runs. Each interval is therefore hashed, classified and
// chained on its OWN segments, and its edges carry its range.
//
// Sharing one hash across the intervals is not a duplicate-output problem but a
// silent deletion: two copies of one area coincide wherever the finer mask did
// not cut them, so the shared segment collects the SAME DRVAL1 twice, reads as
// an interface between two areas of equal depth, and is dropped by the
// `low === high` rule -- taking the cell-ring seam, and the safety contour that
// runs along it, out of every interval at once.
//
// An input carrying none of those properties is one interval and behaves exactly
// as it did before them.

import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from "fs";

import { isFallbackInterval, orientedRings } from "./quilt-geojson.mjs";

const args = process.argv.slice(2);
const depthAreaPaths = [];
const contourPaths = [];
const coveragePaths = [];
const fallbackCoveragePaths = [];
const cellCoveragePaths = [];
const positional = [];

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  if (args[i] === "--depth-area" && args[i + 1]) depthAreaPaths.push(args[++i]);
  else if (args[i] === "--contour" && args[i + 1]) contourPaths.push(args[++i]);
  else if (args[i] === "--coverage" && args[i + 1])
    coveragePaths.push(args[++i]);
  else if (args[i] === "--fallback-coverage" && args[i + 1])
    fallbackCoveragePaths.push(args[++i]);
  else if (args[i] === "--cell-coverage" && args[i + 1])
    cellCoveragePaths.push(args[++i]);
  else positional.push(args[i]);
}

const outputPath = positional[0];
if (!outputPath || depthAreaPaths.length === 0) {
  console.error(
    "Usage: generate-depare-edges <output.geojson> --depth-area <file> [--depth-area <file>] [--contour <file>] [--coverage <file>] [--fallback-coverage <file>] [--cell-coverage <file>]",
  );
  process.exit(1);
}

/** Coordinates round-tripped through GeoJSON text; 9 dp is ~0.1 mm. */
const PRECISION = 9;
/** How close a segment has to sit to the coverage boundary to count as a seam. */
const SEAM_TOLERANCE = 1e-7;
/**
 * The same, for the cell's own M_COVR ring.
 *
 * Looser than SEAM_TOLERANCE because the two geometries do not share edge
 * primitives the way two depth areas of one cell do: M_COVR is its own S-57
 * feature and the depth areas are clipped against the quilt mask, so the
 * coincidence is arithmetic rather than exact. Measured departure on real
 * cells is under 1e-7 degrees; 1e-6 (~0.1 m) is an order of magnitude of
 * headroom and still far below the width of anything drawn on a chart.
 */
const CELL_BOUNDARY_TOLERANCE = 1e-6;
/** Stands in for "the other side is not a depth area". */
const NO_DEPTH = -1;

function readFeatures(path) {
  let text;
  try {
    text = readFileSync(path, "utf8");
  } catch (err) {
    return [];
  }
  if (!text.trim()) return [];
  const parsed = JSON.parse(text);
  return parsed.features ?? [];
}

/** The partition properties a source copy carries; see THE ZOOM PARTITION. */
const QUILT_PROPERTIES = ["_QZMIN", "_QZMAX", "_QFALL"];

/** Those of them a feature actually has, to be copied onto its outputs. */
function quiltRange(properties) {
  const range = {};
  for (const name of QUILT_PROPERTIES) {
    const value = properties?.[name];
    if (value !== null && value !== undefined) range[name] = value;
  }
  return range;
}

/** The interval key of a range. Legacy inputs all share the empty one. */
function quiltKey(range) {
  return QUILT_PROPERTIES.map((name) => range[name] ?? "").join("\u0000");
}

const round = (value) => Number(value.toFixed(PRECISION));
const key = (a, b) => {
  const [x1, y1] = [round(a[0]), round(a[1])];
  const [x2, y2] = [round(b[0]), round(b[1])];
  // Canonical, so the same edge hashes alike whichever way each ring walks it.
  return x1 < x2 || (x1 === x2 && y1 <= y2)
    ? `${x1},${y1}|${x2},${y2}`
    : `${x2},${y2}|${x1},${y1}`;
};

/** Every closed ring of a Polygon/MultiPolygon geometry. */
function* rings(geometry) {
  if (!geometry) return;
  if (geometry.type === "Polygon") yield* geometry.coordinates;
  else if (geometry.type === "MultiPolygon")
    for (const polygon of geometry.coordinates) yield* polygon;
}

/** Only the outer ring of each polygon -- see `coverageIndex`. */
function* exteriorRings(geometry) {
  if (!geometry) return;
  if (geometry.type === "Polygon") {
    if (geometry.coordinates[0]) yield geometry.coordinates[0];
  } else if (geometry.type === "MultiPolygon") {
    for (const polygon of geometry.coordinates)
      if (polygon[0]) yield polygon[0];
  }
}

/** Every segment of a LineString/MultiLineString geometry. */
function* lineSegments(geometry) {
  if (!geometry) return;
  const lines =
    geometry.type === "LineString"
      ? [geometry.coordinates]
      : geometry.type === "MultiLineString"
        ? geometry.coordinates
        : [];
  for (const line of lines) {
    for (let i = 1; i < line.length; i++) yield [line[i - 1], line[i]];
  }
}

// ---- 1. hash every depth-area segment, by zoom interval --------------------

/** Interval key -> { range, segments }, in the order the intervals arrive. */
const intervals = new Map();

function intervalOf(properties) {
  const range = quiltRange(properties);
  const id = quiltKey(range);
  let interval = intervals.get(id);
  if (!interval) {
    interval = { range, segments: new Map() };
    intervals.set(id, interval);
  }
  return interval;
}

for (const path of depthAreaPaths) {
  for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
    const drval1 = Number(feature.properties?.DRVAL1);
    const depth = Number.isFinite(drval1) ? drval1 : NO_DEPTH;
    const { segments } = intervalOf(feature.properties);
    // Canonically wound (filled side on the right of travel), so an emitted
    // edge's direction is deterministic whatever the source winding -- the
    // same invariant every deriveDifferEdges layer holds.
    for (const ring of orientedRings(feature.geometry)) {
      for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
        const a = ring[i - 1];
        const b = ring[i];
        const id = key(a, b);
        const existing = segments.get(id);
        if (existing) existing.depths.push(depth);
        else segments.set(id, { a, b, depths: [depth] });
      }
    }
  }
}

// ---- 2. DEPCNT values, by segment, by zoom interval ------------------------
//
// A contour is copied by the ladder exactly as a depth area is, and a copy
// labels the edges of its own interval: consulting another interval's contours
// would put a VALDCO on an edge whose geometry the clip had moved.

const contourValues = new Map();
for (const path of contourPaths) {
  for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
    const valdco = Number(feature.properties?.VALDCO);
    if (!Number.isFinite(valdco)) continue;
    const id = quiltKey(quiltRange(feature.properties));
    let values = contourValues.get(id);
    if (!values) {
      values = new Map();
      contourValues.set(id, values);
    }
    for (const [a, b] of lineSegments(feature.geometry)) {
      values.set(key(a, b), valdco);
    }
  }
}

// ---- 3. seams: quilt cuts and the cell's own edge ---------------------------

/**
 * Index the rings of a set of coverage polygons as bbox-padded segments.
 *
 * `accept` filters which features contribute -- the cell's own M_COVR carries
 * CATCOV = 2 ("no coverage") rings as well, and a no-coverage ring is not an
 * edge of anything this chart drew.
 *
 * `exteriorOnly` drops the HOLES of the accepted polygons, which is how a
 * no-coverage area actually arrives: as a hole in the CATCOV = 1 polygon with
 * a separate CATCOV = 2 polygon filling it. Indexing that hole puts the
 * no-coverage boundary back into the seam index by the back door and undoes
 * the `accept` filter -- the depth areas that end at the edge of the gap lose
 * their DRVAL_LO, so the safety contour disappears from a boundary that is
 * real. Nothing is lost by dropping holes: the other thing that cuts a hole in
 * a cell's coverage is a higher-INTU chart, and those rings arrive whole on
 * --coverage.
 */
function coverageIndex(
  paths,
  tolerance,
  accept = () => true,
  exteriorOnly = false,
) {
  const index = [];
  for (const path of paths) {
    for (const feature of readFeatures(path)) {
      if (!accept(feature)) continue;
      const walk = exteriorOnly ? exteriorRings : rings;
      for (const ring of walk(feature.geometry)) {
        for (let i = 1; i < ring.length; i++) {
          const [a, b] = [ring[i - 1], ring[i]];
          index.push({
            a,
            b,
            minX: Math.min(a[0], b[0]) - tolerance,
            maxX: Math.max(a[0], b[0]) + tolerance,
            minY: Math.min(a[1], b[1]) - tolerance,
            maxY: Math.max(a[1], b[1]) + tolerance,
          });
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return index;
}

const coverageSegments = coverageIndex(coveragePaths, SEAM_TOLERANCE);

/**
 * The COARSER charts' rings, which only the fallback copy was cut against.
 *
 * Same tolerance as the finer cut, and for the same reason: GEOS keeps the
 * mask's own vertices when it differences, so the cut edge and the ring it was
 * cut along share coordinates exactly.
 */
const fallbackCoverageSegments = coverageIndex(
  fallbackCoveragePaths,
  SEAM_TOLERANCE,
);

/**
 * This cell's own coverage ring: the OUTER ring of its CATCOV = 1 polygons.
 *
 * The attribute arrives as an MVT/GeoJSON integer from ogr2ogr but has been a
 * string in other exports, so it is coerced rather than compared strictly to 1.
 */
const cellSegments = coverageIndex(
  cellCoveragePaths,
  CELL_BOUNDARY_TOLERANCE,
  (feature) => Number(feature.properties?.CATCOV) === 1,
  true,
);

function distanceToSegment(point, a, b) {
  const dx = b[0] - a[0];
  const dy = b[1] - a[1];
  const lengthSquared = dx * dx + dy * dy;
  let t = 0;
  if (lengthSquared > 0) {
    t = ((point[0] - a[0]) * dx + (point[1] - a[1]) * dy) / lengthSquared;
    t = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, t));
  }
  const px = a[0] + t * dx - point[0];
  const py = a[1] + t * dy - point[1];
  return Math.sqrt(px * px + py * py);
}

function near(index, point, tolerance) {
  for (const segment of index) {
    if (point[0] < segment.minX || point[0] > segment.maxX) continue;
    if (point[1] < segment.minY || point[1] > segment.maxY) continue;
    if (distanceToSegment(point, segment.a, segment.b) <= tolerance)
      return true;
  }
  return false;
}

/**
 * Along a ring of `index`: both endpoints AND the midpoint on it.
 *
 * A real coastline that touches a ring at ONE vertex is not a boundary
 * artefact and has to keep DRVAL_LO = -1. The midpoint is tested as well as
 * the endpoints because a long chord across a bay can have both ends on the
 * ring without lying along it.
 */
function alongRing(index, tolerance, a, mid, b) {
  if (index.length === 0) return false;
  return (
    near(index, a, tolerance) &&
    near(index, mid, tolerance) &&
    near(index, b, tolerance)
  );
}

/** On the boundary of another chart's coverage: a quilt cut. */
function onCoverageBoundary(a, mid, b) {
  return alongRing(coverageSegments, SEAM_TOLERANCE, a, mid, b);
}

/** On this cell's own M_COVR ring. */
function onCellBoundary(a, mid, b) {
  return alongRing(cellSegments, CELL_BOUNDARY_TOLERANCE, a, mid, b);
}

/**
 * On the boundary of a COARSER chart's coverage: the fallback copy's cut.
 *
 * Only ever asked about a _QFALL interval -- see case 3 in the header.
 */
function onFallbackBoundary(a, mid, b) {
  return alongRing(fallbackCoverageSegments, SEAM_TOLERANCE, a, mid, b);
}

// ---- 4. chain segments into LineStrings ------------------------------------

function chain(entries) {
  const endpoints = new Map();
  const remaining = new Set(entries);

  const at = (point) => `${round(point[0])},${round(point[1])}`;
  for (const entry of entries) {
    for (const point of [entry.a, entry.b]) {
      const id = at(point);
      const bucket = endpoints.get(id);
      if (bucket) bucket.push(entry);
      else endpoints.set(id, [entry]);
    }
  }

  const lines = [];
  for (const start of entries) {
    if (!remaining.has(start)) continue;
    remaining.delete(start);
    const line = [start.a, start.b];

    // Walk forwards, then backwards, taking the single continuation when the
    // node is a simple one. A junction (three or more edges) ends the chain.
    for (const direction of [1, 0]) {
      for (;;) {
        const tip = direction ? line[line.length - 1] : line[0];
        const candidates = (endpoints.get(at(tip)) ?? []).filter((entry) =>
          remaining.has(entry),
        );
        if (candidates.length !== 1) break;
        const next = candidates[0];
        remaining.delete(next);
        const other = at(next.a) === at(tip) ? next.b : next.a;
        if (direction) line.push(other);
        else line.unshift(other);
      }
    }
    lines.push(line);
  }
  return lines;
}

// ---- 5. classify and emit, one zoom interval at a time ---------------------

const features = [];
let segmentTotal = 0;

for (const [id, interval] of intervals) {
  const contours = contourValues.get(id) ?? new Map();
  // The one interval the coarser roster describes: everything else was cut
  // against finer coverage alone, and a coarser ring across it is real ground.
  const fallback = isFallbackInterval(interval.range);
  segmentTotal += interval.segments.size;

  /** Segments keyed by their attribute signature, for chaining. */
  const byAttributes = new Map();

  for (const [segmentId, segment] of interval.segments) {
    const depths = segment.depths;
    let low;
    let high;
    let seam = false;

    if (depths.length >= 2) {
      low = Math.min(...depths);
      high = Math.max(...depths);
    } else {
      high = depths[0];
      const mid = [
        (segment.a[0] + segment.b[0]) / 2,
        (segment.a[1] + segment.b[1]) / 2,
      ];
      if (
        onCoverageBoundary(segment.a, mid, segment.b) ||
        onCellBoundary(segment.a, mid, segment.b) ||
        (fallback && onFallbackBoundary(segment.a, mid, segment.b))
      ) {
        seam = true;
      } else {
        low = NO_DEPTH;
      }
    }

    // A pair of areas with the same DRVAL1 is not an interface worth drawing.
    if (!seam && low === high) continue;

    const valdco = contours.get(segmentId);
    const signature = `${low ?? "x"}|${high}|${valdco ?? "x"}|${seam ? 1 : 0}`;
    const bucket = byAttributes.get(signature);
    const entry = { a: segment.a, b: segment.b, low, high, valdco, seam };
    if (bucket) bucket.push(entry);
    else byAttributes.set(signature, [entry]);
  }

  for (const entries of byAttributes.values()) {
    const { low, high, valdco, seam } = entries[0];
    for (const line of chain(entries)) {
      features.push({
        type: "Feature",
        geometry: { type: "LineString", coordinates: line },
        properties: {
          ...(low === undefined ? {} : { DRVAL_LO: low }),
          DRVAL_HI: high,
          ...(valdco === undefined ? {} : { VALDCO: valdco }),
          ...(seam ? { SEAM: 1 } : {}),
          // The interval these segments came from, converted to tippecanoe
          // bounds later by bin/stamp-quilt-zooms.
          ...interval.range,
        },
      });
    }
  }
}

writeFileSync(
  outputPath,
  JSON.stringify({ type: "FeatureCollection", features }),
);
const seamSources = [
  ...(coverageSegments.length ? ["quilt cuts"] : []),
  ...(fallbackCoverageSegments.length ? ["fallback cuts"] : []),
  ...(cellSegments.length ? ["cell ring"] : []),
];
console.error(
  `Generated ${features.length} depth-area edges from ${segmentTotal} segments` +
    (seamSources.length ? ` (seams flagged: ${seamSources.join(", ")})` : ""),
);
